<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 750–975</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birka was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> trading town on the island of Björkö in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, flourishing from the mid 8th to the late 10th century. Its merchants traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and goods from the Frankish, Byzantine and Abbasid worlds, and the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> preached there around 830. With nearby Hovgården it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birka.jpg" alt="Birka" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger.Ellgaard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birka_Ansgar_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Birka stood on the island of Björkö in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a>, 30 kilometres west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. It was founded around 750 by a king or by merchants seeking to control trade, and is one of the earliest urban settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. It was the Baltic link in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> trade route through Ladoga and Novgorod to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. It most likely offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, iron goods and craft products for materials from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Western Asia. Furs came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and combs of reindeer antler were important. The population has been estimated at 500 to 1,000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimbert">Rimbert</a>'s Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> says Ansgar travelled to Birka in 829 on a mission from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> and founded the first known Christian congregation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 831. Its attempt to convert people from the Norse religion was unsuccessful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> says Archbishop Unni died at Birka in 936. Birka was abandoned in the latter half of the 10th century, around the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a> was founded and became the main trading centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Mälaren</a> area. The reasons are disputed: Gotland was better placed for Rus' and Byzantine trade, and Neil Kent has speculated that an enemy assault may have destroyed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>No texts survive from Birka itself, and no Norse source names it. The settlement's location was lost for centuries, and the Chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> first claimed in 1450 that Björkö was Birka. Johan Hadorph attempted excavations in the late 17th century. Hjalmar Stolpe came in the late 19th century to study insects in amber, found so much that he suspected a trading post, and excavated between 1871 and 1895.</p><p>The remains cover about 7 hectares and include a hill fort called Borgen and more than 3,000 graves, both cremations and burials in coffins or chamber graves. Goods in the graves include glass, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> pottery, Chinese silk and Byzantine embroidery with fine gold thread. Most coins found are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Ten small silver crosses and 27 pendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>'s hammer show that both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and traditional beliefs were present. Birka and Hovgården on the neighbouring island of Adelsö have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Viking Age, Swedish traders and warriors, often called Varangians or Rus, travelled the rivers of Russia to Byzantium and the Caliphate, and <b>Birka</b> on Lake Mälaren was a major trading town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A warrior’s grave at <b>Birka</b> in Sweden, long assumed to be a man’s, was shown by DNA in 2017 to be a woman’s, though what this means is debated.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In trading towns such as Hedeby, <b>Birka</b> and Kaupang lived craftsmen and merchants, and free but landless servants (húskarlar) worked on other men’s farms.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Birka</b>, founded in the 8th century on Lake Mälaren, linked the kingdom to trade routes east to Rus' and the Byzantine and Abbasid worlds.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
